    "q-343016": {
      "dom_id": "step-q343016",
      "name": "Question 5",
      "problem": "Find the real solution to the equation $\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x+5\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x=5.$\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}",
      "worked_solution": "The definitions of $\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x$ and $\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x$ are\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x=\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{x}+{e}^{-x}),\\,\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x=\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{x}-{e}^{-x}).\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above definitions into the equation $\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x+5\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x=5,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{x}+{e}^{-x})+5\\cdot (\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{x}-{e}^{-x})) & =5 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}({e}^{x}+{e}^{-x})+\\frac{5}{2}({e}^{x}-{e}^{-x}) & =5 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}{e}^{x}+\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{-x}+\\frac{5}{2}{e}^{x}-\\frac{5}{2}{e}^{-x} & =5 \\\\ 3{e}^{x}-2{e}^{-x} & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we multiply both sides of the equation by ${e}^{x},$ and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x}\\cdot (3{e}^{x}-2{e}^{-x}) & ={e}^{x}\\cdot 5 \\\\ 3{e}^{2x}-2 & =5{e}^{x} \\\\ 3{e}^{2x}-5{e}^{x}-2 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a quadratic equation in ${e}^{x}$ that we can factor as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(3{e}^{x}+1)({e}^{x}-2) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy the zero-product property, we have to solve two equations:\n\n$$\n3{e}^{x}+1=0,\\,{e}^{x}-2=0.\n$$\n\nSolving the two equations above for $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{e}^{x}+1 & =0 &  & \\, & {e}^{x}-2 & =0 \\\\ 3{e}^{x} & =-1 &  & \\, & {e}^{x} & =2 \\\\ {e}^{x} & =-\\frac{1}{3} &  & \\, & x & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThere are no real values of $x$ for which ${e}^{x}=-\\frac{1}{3}.$ Therefore, the only real solution to the equation is\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2).\n$$",
